<p>Aristotle: Virtue, the Good Life, and What We Owe Our Own Character</p>
<p><img src="/phil2306/assets/images/_the_school_of_athens__by_raffaello_sanzio_da_urbino.jpg" alt="Raphael, from The School of Athens (1509–1511) showing Aristotle holding the Nicomachean Ethics. Aristotle gestures downward toward the earth, signaling his emphasis on the practical and empirical." width="860" height="340" loading="lazy" class="page-hero-image"></p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>The oldest systematic ethics in the Western tradition, and still one of the sharpest. Aristotle wants to know what a good human life looks like, and he thinks the answer runs through character.</p>
</div></div>
<div class="accordion">
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">1</span>
    What this unit is about
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Ask anyone what they want out of life and, sooner or later, you will hear some version of the same answer: to be happy. Aristotle thinks this answer is correct as far as it goes, but he does not think most people know what they are talking about when they say it. Most of us treat happiness as a feeling, or a run of good luck, or the result of getting what we want. Aristotle argues that happiness, properly understood, is none of these things. It is an activity: the activity of a human being living well, over the course of a whole life, by exercising the capacities that make us the kind of creature we are.</p>
<p>That is the heart of the <em>Nicomachean Ethics</em>. In Book I, Aristotle asks what the final goal of human life is and argues his way to the concept of <em>eudaimonia</em>, usually translated as "happiness" or "flourishing." In Book II, he argues that eudaimonia is achieved through virtue, and that virtues are habits of character acquired by practice, not gifts of nature or lessons we can simply be taught. In Book III, he turns to the conditions under which we hold people responsible for what they do, distinguishing voluntary action from involuntary action and setting out his account of choice.</p>
<p>These are not museum pieces. The questions Aristotle is asking are still the questions we ask when we think about what kind of person we want to become, which actions we are on the hook for, and what it would mean to live a life we could look back on without regret.</p>
<div class="callout callout--connection"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">➡️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>LOOKING AHEAD →</p>
<p>Aristotle grounds ethics in human nature: morality emerges from what we are and what we are for. Every thinker after him in this course is, in some sense, responding to that move. Aquinas will inherit it and baptize it; Kant will replace it with reason; Mill will replace it with consequences; Nietzsche will try to tear it down; de Beauvoir will ask what ethics looks like when there is no fixed human nature to ground it in.</p>
</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">2</span>
    Before you begin
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>Aristotle is not a difficult writer in the way Kant is. His sentences are mostly short and direct. What makes him hard for modern readers is that he assumes a way of thinking about the world that we no longer share: a world in which every living thing has a purpose built into its nature, and in which asking what a thing is for is as natural as asking what it is made of.</p>
<p>You do not need to accept this worldview to read Aristotle carefully. But it helps to notice when he is using it, because the bulk of his argument depends on it.</p>
<p>The readings in this unit come from the <em>Nicomachean Ethics</em>, most likely named after his son Nicomachus, who may have edited the lecture notes after Aristotle's death. That is what you are reading: lecture notes, not a polished book. Expect the prose to feel like notes in places. Expect Aristotle to circle back to a point rather than state it cleanly the first time.</p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>READING TIP</p>
<p>Read each book once quickly, then again more slowly. The first pass is to get the shape of the argument. The second pass is where the work gets done. Do not take notes on the first pass; you will waste time on sentences that turn out to be minor.</p>
</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="accordion__item">
  <button class="accordion__header" aria-expanded="false" type="button">
    <span class="accordion__number">3</span>
    Learning objectives
    <span class="accordion__chevron" aria-hidden="true">▼</span>
  </button>
  <div class="accordion__content">
    <div class="accordion__body">
<p>By the end of this unit, you should be able to:</p>
<ul>
<li>Explain what Aristotle means by <em>eudaimonia</em> and why he thinks it is the final end of human life.</li>
<li>Reconstruct Aristotle's function argument (the <em>ergon</em> argument) for the human good.</li>
<li>Describe what a virtue is, in Aristotle's account, and explain the difference between intellectual and moral virtue.</li>
<li>Apply the doctrine of the mean to a concrete virtue, identifying its flanking vices of excess and deficiency.</li>
<li>Explain what makes an action voluntary or involuntary on Aristotle's account, and how ignorance and compulsion excuse.</li>
<li>Evaluate Aristotle's claim that virtue is acquired through habituation rather than instruction.</li>
</ul>
